Your code is close, but it's missing a base case to stop the recursion. In recursion, you need a condition that will make the recursive call stop. 

Here's the fix as pseudocode, but with exact C++ syntax:

```
// Define a base case to stop recursion when n is 0 or 1
int fact(int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1) {
        // Return 1 when n is 0 or 1, since 0! and 1! are both 1
        return 1;
    } else {
        // Otherwise, return n * fact(n - 1)
        return n * fact(n - 1);
    }
}
```

This way, when `n` is 0 or 1, the function will stop calling itself and start returning the result.

